      "math_academy_id": "e-1075",
      "problem": "Simplify the  expression $\\dfrac {p^7} {p^2}  \\cdot p^3 .$",
      "worked_solution": "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(7-2)}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(5+3)} & = \\\\ {p}^{8}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}={p}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
